            "sources": {
                "parts": "By the model (bin/lib/hulls/barkcanoe.py): her bark 5 mm thick over her laths of 1 by 6 cm white cedar",
                "shell": "Balance: bark half a centimetre thick, lined by the cedar sheathing, 1/8 in thick (native-art-in-canada.com/birchbarkcanoes.html), so a blow splits both where it lies, by the model the share of her sides and her bottom the sheathing covers (bin/lib/hulls/wounds.py). A breaker bends bark and sheathing together between the ribs. A knock, a ram or a blast splits the bark and the sheathing where it lands, as a point blow does, and the rib behind takes the rest, 1 by 6 cm of cedar breaking by its impact bending work across its section, or both ribs when it lands between them. Bark is the weak part: Adney and Chapelle (The Bark Canoes and Skin Boats of North America, 1964) quote LaHontan that the canoes were easily damaged and their bark mended at the end of each day, and Malecite canoes wore shoes to keep rocks and snags off the bark",
                "runsRapids": "She draws almost no water and rides over the rocks (docs/design.md). A stub, as the rapids' strike is (rapids.json): until rapids are worked out from draft (#260), which hull runs them is this flag"
            },
