            ],
            "sources": {
                "time": "A held action lasts the real time of that one piece of work over sixty: game time runs 30 times faster than real time, and boat work is halved again",
                "barkSeconds": "Scoring a five-metre sheet along the trunk and around it, then prying and peeling it whole: some 20 to 40 minutes (an estimate; robin-wood.co.uk, 2014, describes the work but gives no time)",
                "slashes": "Her bark is slashed from its edge in to the frame where it narrows, so that it turns up round it without buckling (Adney and Chapelle 1964): one slash a short cut through bark, about a minute (an estimate)",
                "ends": "Each end is trimmed along the curve of its stem and the sheet's surplus cut off, as her ends are shaped once the frame is raised (Adney and Chapelle 1964): a few minutes an end (an estimate)",
                "frame": "Fitting one lath, about three minutes: a steamed rib has to be driven in within a couple of minutes, before it cools (robin-wood.co.uk, birch bark canoe build, 2014)",
                "lashing": "One rope is ten metres of lashing, some fifty turns, each through a hole made with an awl: about a quarter of an hour (an estimate). The canoe takes as much as her gunwales and stems need, groups of eight to ten turns two inches apart (Adney & Chapelle 1964), counted by her shape's builder",
                "pitch": "About a quart of pitch for a canoe (forums.wcha.org, bark pitch questions), one vanilla pot of pitch, a quarter litre to each of her four seams; pitching them all about half an hour (an estimate)",
                "ribs": "Ribs bent in wet hold their bend once dry, a couple of days (the Lac du Flambeau build, canoe.csumc.wisc.edu): their laths dry as they would in the bundle",
                "framePart": "Her frame is the wood of her laths: its share and the weight it was set for are white cedar's, its strength and density are those of the wood split"
            }
        },
